><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Jaiteh</surname><given-names>LES</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Antenatal care in The Gambia: Misses opportunity for information, education and communication</article-title><source>BMC Pregnancy and Childbirth</source><year>2009</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>9</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18325122</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/1471-2393-8-9</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Caelli</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Ray</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Mill</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>"Clear as mud: towards greater clarity in generic qualitative research"</article-title><source>Int J Qual Methods</source><year>2003</year><volume>2</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>24</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ceesay</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Casals-Pascual</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Erskine</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Anya</surname><given-names>SE</given-names></name><name><surname>Duah</surname><given-names>NO</given-names></name><name><surname>Fulford</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Sesay</surname><given-names>SS</given-names></name><name><surname>Abubakar</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Dunyo</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Sey</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name><name><surname>Palmer</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Fofana</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Corrah</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Bojang</surname><given-names>KA</given-names></name><name><surname>Whittle</surname><given-names>HC</given-names></name><name><surname>Greenwood</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name><name><surname>Conway</surname><given-names>DJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Changes in malaria indices between 1999 and 2007 in The Gambia: a retrospective analysis</article-title><source>Lancet</source><year>2008</year><volume>372</volume><fpage>1545</fpage><lpage>1554</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18984187</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0140-6736(08)61654-2</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mubyazi</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Bloch</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Kamugisha</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Kitua</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Ijumba</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Intermittent preventive treatment of malaria during pregnancy: a qualitative study of knowledge, attitudes and practices of district health managers, antenatal care staff and pregnant women in Korogwe District, North-Eastern Tanzania</article-title><source>Malar J</source><year>2005</year><volume>4</volume><fpage>31</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16033639</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/1475-2875-4-31</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ouma</surname><given-names>PO</given-names></name><name><surname>Van Eijk</surname><given-names>AM</given-names></name><name><surname>Hamel</surname><given-names>MJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Sikuku</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Odhiambo</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Munguti</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Ayisi</surname><given-names>JG</given-names></name><name><surname>Kager</surname><given-names>PA</given-names></name><name><surname>Slutsker</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The effect of health care worker training on use of intermittent preventive treatment for malaria in pregnancy in rural western Kenya</article-title><source>Trop Med Int Health</source><year>2007</year><volume>12</volume><fpage>953</fpage><lpage>961</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17697090</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Renne</surname><given-names>EP</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The pregnancy that doesn't stay: the practice and perception of abortion by Ekiti Yoruba women</article-title><source>Soc Sci Med</source><year>1996</year><volume>42</volume><fpage>483</fpage><lpage>494</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8643975</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0277-9536(95)00171-9</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bonnet</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><source>Corps biologique corps social &#x02013; Procr&#x000e9;ation et maladies de l'enfant en pays mossi Burkina Faso</source><year>1988</year><publisher-name>Paris, France: Editions de l'ORSTOM</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shaikh</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Maguire D</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Family planning, contraception and abortion in Islam</article-title><source>Sacred Choices: The Case for Contraception and Abortion in World Religions</source><year>2003</year><publisher-name>Oxford University Press</publisher-name><fpage>105</fpage><lpage>128</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citatio